As you've confirmed WRT wands of teleport yesterday,
your memories had not given you an accurate picture;
it's hard to take that as an even faintly reliable
source of what's a normal or abnormal amount. Sadly.

(Digging are mostly plentiful. There are less if you
avoid or genocide the minotauri as one non-negligible
source. But I don't think that digging was an issue
here.)

But this started, as far as I recall, by the issue
of escaping from the dungeon quickly, using teleport
and cursed _gain level_ which are, as claimed, all
available in abundance. While this is true in many
games for teleport, it looks like a myth WRT gain
level. How do you prevent hostile monsters from just
drinking their potions before you even hit them the
first time? - Those monsters that get such potions
just drink them! There's the notable exception with
dragons; if you kill sufficient amounts of dragons
this seems a good source for potions. But while in
some games those dragons appear plentiful in other
games you don't meet too many. WRT the healing
potions monsters drink them when getting low on HPs.
So the key is being able to kill them on average
with at most two hits (one hit if they get below
some HP value); not all characters can do that. A
solution for that are instant death attacks like
rubber chickens, but those are not always available
when you want them. WRT amounts of healing potions
for alchemy I've done the analysis from the NAO
data; the claim that had been made was wrong as well
as it turned out.

So all boils down to the potions of gain level.
And here I am interested where from and how you get
more than the probability numbers indicate, or how
you can make the monsters not drink them on first
occasion in the case the monsters already have them
in their inventories.

If there's some substantial knowledge about possible
improvement of game-play I'd really like to hear and
learn. What had been presented in the past was only
"there just is enough available", and "you are doing
something wrong" - needless to say that such claims
aren't worth a Zorkmid.

